\subsection RadiationModelValidation Field Validation

1 - <b>Vineyard Canopy Light Interception</b>

   <a href="">Ponce de León et al. (2025)</a> conducted a field validation of the Helios radiation model in five different vineyard designs. Measurements consisted of differential measurements of all-wave solar radiation flux above and below the canopy. Results are summarized in the figure below.

   \image html vineyard_radiation_validation.jpg width=800
   <b>Figure.</b> Measured and simulated radiation flux reaching the inter-row in the PAR and NIR band (\f$R_{inter-row}\f$) in five different vineyard designs. This includes split grape canopy cases with rows orientated NE-SW (Site 1) and NW-SE (Site 2) at Barrelli Creek, Cloverdale CA; a double vertical grape canopy case with rows oriented E-W (Site 3) and a split canopy case with rows oriented E-W (Site 4) at Madera, CA; and a split grape canopy with rows oriented E-W in Lodi, CA (Site 5). Measurements and simulations were conducted every 1 min (Sites 1, 2, and 4) and every 15 min (Sites 3 and 5). The dashed magenta line is the measurement, and the solid black like is the Helios simulation. \f$Q_0\f$ indicates the incoming radiation flux.

2 - <b>Orchard Canopy Light Interception</b>

   Ponce de León et al. (under review) conducted a field validation of the Helios radiation model in an almond and olive orchard canopy. Measurements consisted of differential measurements of all-wave solar radiation flux above and below the canopy. Results are summarized in the figure below.

   \image html orchard_radiation_validation.png width=600
    <b>Figure.</b> Measured and simulated 15-minute radiation flux reaching the inter-row in the PAR and NIR band (\f$R_{inter-row}\f$, W m<sup>-2</sup>) in an (a) almond orchard with rows orientated: NE-SW at Solano, CA on July 18, 2023 and in an (b) olive orchard with rows oriented N-S at Glenn, CA on July 18, 2024.

\section EnergyBalanceTemperature Energy Balance Surface Temperature Model Validation

\subsection EnergyBalanceTemperatureVerification Verification Tests

An important verification test of the energy balance model, aside from verifying energy balance closure, is to verify that the model satisifes the second law of thermodynamics. This is tested by setting the absorbed radiation flux of all elements in a simulated canopy scene to zero, and setting the longwave flux from the sky equal to \f$\sigma T^4\f$, where \f$T\f$ is an arbitrary temperature in Kelvin. After running the energy balance, all elements in the scene should have the same computed temperature of \f$T\f$. This test is implemented in `plugins/energybalance/tests/selfTest.cpp`, and is explored in more detail in <a href="http://dx.doi.org/doi:10.1016/j.ecolmodel.2017.11.022">Bailey (2018)</a>.

\subsection EnergyBalanceTemperatureValidation Field Validation

1 - <b>Grape Berry Temperature Measurements</b>

<a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.agrformet.2021.108431">Ponce de León and Bailey (2021)</a> validated the energy balance model against grape berry temperature measured with fine-wire thermocouples in four Cabernet Sauvignon vineyards, each with a different trellis system: VSP and Wye at Davis, CA (flat terrain), and Goblet and Unilateral at Napa, CA (terraced on a 30° east-facing slope). Thermocouples were inserted into berries in exposed clusters on both the east and west side of the vine, and a weather station adjacent to each vine supplied the ambient forcing so that only berry-scale transfer was being tested.

This is a demanding test of the model because berry temperature is dominated by the sun-shade transitions produced by the surrounding leaves. Measurements showed that shaded berries closely track air temperature, while intermittent direct beam exposure can drive berries more than 10 °C above ambient. Reproducing this behavior requires the 3D geometry of the vine to be resolved accurately enough to predict when each individual berry is shaded. Agreement was very good across all four trellis systems, with \f$R^2\f$ between 0.94 and 0.97, index of agreement between 0.98 and 0.99, and NRMSE between 4.6% and 8.5%. The largest instantaneous errors occurred at rapid sun-shade transitions, where a modeled transition could lag the measured one by about an hour due to small errors in the assumed position of individual berries and leaves.

The study also demonstrated the importance of the berry heat storage term in the energy balance. Without it, simulated berry temperature responded too quickly to changes in absorbed radiation and consistently over- and under-shot the measured extremes.

\image html berry_temperature_validation.jpg width=700
<b>Figure.</b> Daily course of simulated and average measured grape berry temperature in the east and west side of the vine for trellis systems (a) VSP, (b) Wye, (c) Goblet and (d) Unilateral.

2 - <b>Sorghum Leaf Temperature Measurements</b>

<a href="https://doi.org/10.34133/plantphenomics.0169">Mayanja et al. (2024)</a> used Helios to analyze the sensitivity of leaf temperature to stomatal conductance, and as part of that work validated simulated leaf temperature against field measurements in four diverse sorghum lines from the sorghum Nested Association Mapping panel (PI656023, PI534133, PI656111, PI533766) grown at the UC Davis Agronomy fields. For each genotype, 30 paired measurements of leaf temperature and stomatal conductance were collected on healthy, fully expanded sunlit leaves using a LI-600 porometer, and the canopy geometry used in the simulation was measured directly in the field.

The model reproduced both the genotype-averaged leaf temperature and stomatal conductance, with a root mean square error of 0.95 °C for leaf temperature and 0.011 mol m<sup>-2</sup> s<sup>-1</sup> for stomatal conductance. As the figure below shows, the simulated spread across genotypes is larger than the measured spread, which is expected since the simulation resolves the full leaf-to-leaf variability within the canopy rather than a sample of sunlit leaves.

\image html sorghum_leaf_temperature_validation.png width=400
<b>Figure.</b> Measured and simulated leaf temperature (\f$\hat{T}_l\f$, °C) averaged across each of four sorghum genotypes. The hat operator denotes a spatial average, and error bars denote the standard deviation across samples.

3 - <b>Redbud Tree Leaf Temperature Measurements</b>

An additional validation was performed for an eastern redbud (<i>Cercis canadensis</i>) tree, in which simulated leaf temperature was compared against an ensemble of LI-600 porometer measurements collected across a single day (September 4, 2023). Because many leaves were sampled at each time, the measurements capture the spread in leaf temperature produced by variation in leaf orientation and shading within the crown, rather than a single canopy mean.

The model reproduces the diurnal course of canopy-average leaf temperature closely, with a mean difference of 0.05 °C, an RMSE of 0.48 °C, and \f$R^2 = 0.99\f$ between the measured and modeled moving averages. The instantaneous scatter shows the expected behavior: the measured distribution is wider than the modeled one during the morning, when a few strongly sunlit leaves reach temperatures several degrees above the canopy average.

\image html redbud_leaf_temperature_validation.png width=600
<b>Figure.</b> Comparison between simulated leaf temperature and an ensemble of measurements using a LICOR LI-600 porometer across a day.

